# Theme Approaches — Four Transplants of the Delivery/Connection Loop

Every approach keeps the identical mechanical skeleton (orders → loadout → hazardous traversal → grading/likes → station stars → network growth → buildable infrastructure → decay/maintenance → echo porters). What differs is the fiction that carries it, and how legibly each fiction expresses the two things the loop lives on: **connection makes land visibly safer** and **before/after contrast**.

Scoring criteria (from the research docs): (1) connection→safety visible on the map itself, (2) natural home for all six feedback channels, (3) build-sink ladder from pocket tool to communal megaproject, (4) hazards that threaten cargo rather than health, (5) top-down 2D feasibility with high art payoff in canvas.

## A. WAYLIGHT — light against the Grey

A mountain valley drowned in a hazardous mist (the Grey) that erodes cargo and hides wraiths. Isolated stations hold out around dying light-towers. The porter carries beacon cores; every station connected ignites its waylight and pushes the Grey back in a radius that grows with the station's star level. Lit land: no wraiths, gentler rain, faster stamina recovery.

Loop mapping: chiral network → light coverage % (the map literally brightens); timefall → greyfall rain (container + structure decay); BTs → wraiths in deep mist that yank cargo to snags; MULEs → scrapper camps that chase cargo carriers — and convert into friendly rest stops when the light reaches their camp (connection literally pacifies the land); roads → paver stations fed by hauled materials; ziplines late.

Distinct hook: network % is not a number on a screen — it is the screen. Every connection changes what the whole map looks like, and a wraith chasing you across the boundary burns away in the light.

Risks: needs strong glow/fog compositing in canvas — standard technique, low risk.

## B. TIDEBOUND — the drowned post

Archipelago after a flood; stations on peaks; a tide cycle floods and exposes causeways on a timer. Cargo runs happen in tide windows; causeways and ferry lines are the build sink; signal towers are the network.

Distinct hook: tides turn route planning into scheduling.
Risks: the body-load traversal game (balance, slope, stamina) mostly dissolves in water crossings; boats shift the fantasy from porter to pilot; a tide timer frustrates more often than it tenses.

## C. HIGH POST — the last mail line

1900s alpine mail service under a volcanic winter. The connection is literal: the porter hauls telegraph wire station-to-station, strings cableways (ziplines), digs snow shelters. Blizzard icing is the decay force; avalanche slopes and wolf packs are the hazards.

Distinct hook: connection has weight — you carry the wire.
Risks: the safety transformation is weather-only (no faction to pacify, nothing that hunts cargo); no dark-to-light visual arc, so network growth reads as a stat rather than a changed world.

## D. ROOTWAY — seeds through the spore

Colony arcologies on an exoplanet choked by spore fog. Deliver germinal cores; purifier trees bloom and cleanse the air in a radius; cleansed land turns green and safe. Fungal stalkers hunt in the fog.

Distinct hook: terraforming palette shift, grey → verdant.
Risks: structurally approach A in different clothes; "clean air radius" reads weaker at a glance than light against dark; convincing alien flora is the most expensive art target of the four in canvas.

## Scores (5 = best serves the loop)

| Criterion                            | A      | B      | C      | D      |
|--------------------------------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|
| Connection→safety visible on the map | 5      | 4      | 3      | 5      |
| Six feedback channels fit            | 5      | 4      | 3      | 4      |
| Build-sink ladder                    | 5      | 5      | 4      | 4      |
| Cargo-first hazards                  | 5      | 3      | 3      | 4      |
| 2D feasibility + art payoff          | 5      | 3      | 4      | 3      |
| **Total**                            | **25** | **19** | **17** | **20** |

## Selection: A — WAYLIGHT

The loop's strongest single feeling is before/after contrast, and A puts that contrast on every pixel of the play space: dark map → lit map, wraith mist → safe paved road. It carries both DS threat archetypes (a supernatural cargo-thief and a human one), and scrapper pacification makes "connecting people makes the land safer" a literal rule of the simulation rather than flavor. Greyfall carries the decay/maintenance loop. C's best idea is stolen into it: beacon cores are heavy, bulky cargo, so every connection is itself a hard delivery — the connection has weight.
